WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.620
Speaker 1: OK, let's get into audience questions
for the week, some some good ones, I must

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:05.029
say, I'm impressed with the audience.

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:11.380
First question, David, what happens to drug
trafficking and the illegal drug trade if

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.360
all drugs are decriminalized or legalized?

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.500
So these are really two questions.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:24.289
What happens to drug trafficking with decriminalization
is very different than what happens to drug

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:26.170
trafficking with legalization.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:33.900
So if we just see decriminalization of drugs
in terms of possession, you're not going to

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.020
see a drastic change to trafficking.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.750
In that case, the cartels won't really need
to change anything in order to continue being

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:41.790
profitable.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:47.550
Operationally, I don't think that they would
see any anything major change.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:52.840
But you would start to maybe see some changes
when it comes to American institutions.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:57.260
Maybe at the prison level you would see some
differences, although I don't think they would

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:58.870
be as big as some people think.

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.690
I'm going to get back to that.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:11.550
You would probably see more people suffering
from addiction willing to go and seek treatment

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.680
because of this word decriminalization.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.100
It's no longer a criminal thing.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:22.950
But I think for the most part, from a dealer
standpoint, from a cartel standpoint, from

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:27.480
a gang standpoint, and we know about the overlap
between drugs, drugs and gangs you would see

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.560
operating.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:34.960
You would see them operating basically the
same way they do, although maybe they would

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:42.409
shift a little bit to areas where you could
leverage something that is still completely

00:01:42.409 --> 00:01:49.580
criminal rather than decriminalized, but still
not really OK.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:56.310
And remember that when we talk about decriminalization
and legalization, we're talking about possession

00:01:56.310 --> 00:02:04.950
for use and the idea of when even in cases
of legalization, you still need to be licensed.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:11.390
I mean, look at cigarets as an example, cigarets
which are legal.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:17.980
You still see a black market for cigarets
and you still see people trying to pay less

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:24.340
for them by going obtaining them in ways other
than the way that the government has established

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:26.610
through regulation should be done.

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:34.090
Now, if you actually saw legalization, I think
that that would take a big bite out of the

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:35.090
black market.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:39.590
I think you would see if there was really
full legalization where state government or

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:43.990
federal government or both says, as has been
done with cannabis and a lot of places already,

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:45.490
this is now legal.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:48.260
As long as you're licensed to produce and
sell it.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:53.340
There are some limits in terms of how much
any individual can have or grow.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:56.980
And you need to be twenty one and you need
to be paying taxes in this way, in that type

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:01.840
of thing, you would still have a black market,
which would likely be cheaper and you still

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.280
see that.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.120
But it might actually impact the level of
violence to some degree.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:12.210
You I think that you would actually see a
decrease in the amount of violence, but it

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:17.069
would be less violence related to the production
and distribution of that substance.

00:03:17.069 --> 00:03:19.599
You would still have gangs and cartels.

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:25.540
My expectation would be if you legalize more
and more drugs, you'd see gangs and cartels

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:33.540
go in the direction of human trafficking,
organ trafficking, extortion, kidnaping, protection.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:38.319
And the sort of example or model for that
is look at the United States during alcohol

00:03:38.319 --> 00:03:41.400
prohibition in the early 20th century.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:46.190
It created a different criminal world.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:51.510
And then when it was really legalized, it's
not like criminality went away.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.019
It was a switch.

00:03:53.019 --> 00:04:02.459
We've seen mafias switch from and to, you
know, from alcohol to gambling or prostitution

00:04:02.459 --> 00:04:04.459
or whatever the case may be.

00:04:04.459 --> 00:04:07.410
So then let's talk about the prison thing.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:15.130
With decriminalization of personal use amounts
alone, I actually am not sure that American

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:18.019
prison populations would dramatically decrease.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:20.049
And what I mean by that is the following.

00:04:20.049 --> 00:04:27.069
Yes, some people do end up in prison simply
for possession of personal use.

00:04:27.069 --> 00:04:33.259
Drugs for the most part, like overwhelmingly
people that are in prison for drugs are in

00:04:33.259 --> 00:04:42.759
prison because they either were involved in
the trade and had amounts that are far bigger

00:04:42.759 --> 00:04:46.080
than what would be decriminalized or legalized.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:52.599
And what we're seeing already at the state
level bears this out or it's drugs connected

00:04:52.599 --> 00:04:54.539
to other crimes as well.

00:04:54.539 --> 00:04:59.509
So I think if you were to legalize, decriminalize
and say.

00:04:59.509 --> 00:05:08.039
Everybody in prison simply for a personal
possession drug charge is being led out, the

00:05:08.039 --> 00:05:14.030
US would still be very, very high in terms
of per capita incarceration globally.

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:19.889
And I don't know that simply decriminalizing
or legalizing personal use amounts is actually

00:05:19.889 --> 00:05:23.710
going to be the big improvement from a criminal
justice standpoint, as some people believe

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:24.710
that it's going to be.

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:25.710
Those are the numbers I found.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:26.969
If you disagree, let me know

